<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Raining. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Use basic words or phrases to identify time words. Interactive: Exchange simple questions and answers about the current day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can count weather features up to 20, with help (e.g., "Let's count the clouds! One, two, three."). Can answer simple questions about the weather, using one- or two-word responses (e.g., responding "cold" when asked, "What is the weather today?"). Can express simple feelings or reactions to weather using words and gestures (e.g., saying "I'm hot." while fanning themselves or "I'm cold." with a shivering gesture).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts the class by singing a simple "Weather Song" (e.g., "What's the weather, what's the weather? Is it sunny? Is it rainy?"). The teacher then holds up large, colorful flashcards of different weather conditions (sun, rain, clouds, wind, snow) and asks students, "What's this?" encouraging one-word choral responses. The teacher specifically asks, "Is it windy?" while showing the "windy" flashcard and having students blow air to imitate wind. This activates prior knowledge and gets students speaking familiar vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up the "sunny" flashcard and says clearly, "Sunny! It is sunny." emphasizing the /s/ sound. Then, the teacher holds up the "raining" flashcard and says, "Raining! It is raining." emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models the initial sound of /w/ in "weather" by asking, "What is the <b>w</b>eather?" with exaggerated mouth movements. Students repeat chorally after each new word/phrase. The teacher also models simple reactions like "I'm hot!" (fanning self) and "I'm cold!" (shivering) with appropriate gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a "sunny" flashcard: "Is it rainy?" (Students respond: "No! Sunny!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the sun flashcard: "Is it sun?" (Students respond: "Yes! Sun!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated simple dialogue on a large poster or projected image, depicting two children talking about the weather for "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Child 1: "Hello! What is the weather today?" (pointing outside or to a window)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Child 2: "Hello! It is sunny! Nice weather today!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Child 1: "Oh! I'm hot!" (fanning self)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Next scene shows rainy weather)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Child 1: "Oh no! It is raining!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Child 2: "Yes! I'm cold!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking basic questions, such as "What is the weather today?" to learn about daily conditions and describing simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.") and reactions (e.g., "I'm hot." or "I'm cold.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to the characters and actions. Students listen and watch.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks simple gist questions: "Is it raining at the start?" (Students shake heads: "No! Sunny!"). "Is the boy cold when it rains?" (Students nod: "Yes! Cold!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows weather flashcards and asks students to point to the character who said "I'm hot!" or "I'm cold!" based on the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Present continuous ("It is raining."), Subject + verb + adjective ("It is sunny."), and WH-questions ("What is the weather today?") with vocabulary such as "sunny", "rainy", "cloudy", "windy", "warm", "cold", "hot", and number words 1-20.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Repetition Drill:</b> The teacher holds up a weather flashcard (e.g., "sunny") and says, "It is sunny." Students repeat chorally, then individually. The teacher repeats with "It is raining," "It is cloudy," "It is windy." For "It is raining," students also make rain sounds. The teacher then adds reaction phrases: "It is sunny. I'm hot!" (Students repeat and fan themselves). "It is raining. I'm cold!" (Students repeat and shiver). This drills pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion & Substitution:</b> The teacher presents a sentence frame on the board or orally: "It is ______." The teacher shows a weather flashcard (e.g., "windy") and students complete the sentence: "It is windy." The teacher then prompts with, "What is the weather today?" Students respond with a one- or two-word answer (e.g., "Sunny!" or "It's cold."). The teacher also guides counting: "Look at the clouds! How many clouds?" and models counting up to 5, then students chime in.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Partner Question and Answer:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 weather flashcards. Student A picks a flashcard (without showing it) and asks Student B, "What is the weather today?" Student B guesses, "Is it sunny?" Student A responds "Yes, it is!" or "No, it isn't. Try again!" until Student B guesses correctly and then says, "It is [weather]." They then switch roles. This encourages interaction and application of the target questions and answers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Weather Reporter" Pair Role-Play Game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students remain in their pairs. Each pair receives a large "weather scene" drawing (e.g., a paper plate with a weather drawing on it - one might be sunny with 5 clouds, another rainy with 8 raindrops, etc.). These drawings can include multiple features to count.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A acts as the "Weather Reporter" and describes the weather on their paper plate: "Hello! It is [sunny/raining/cloudy]! Look! I see [number] clouds/raindrops/suns!" They also state a feeling: "I'm hot!" or "I'm cold!" using gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B acts as the "Audience" and asks a follow-up question: "What is the weather like?" or "Are you hot or cold?". Student B also helps count the features if needed ("Let's count the clouds! One, two...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging natural interaction, focusing on fluency and communication over perfect grammar. The teacher reminds students to use gestures for "hot" and "cold."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Role:</b> Facilitate and prompt, e.g., "How many clouds do you see?", "What else can you say?", "Tell your friend if you are hot or cold."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Weather Reporter" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Weather Reporter" role-play in pairs, showcasing their ability to describe weather, ask and answer questions, and express feelings. The teacher observes specific students, noting their performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /s/ (sunny) and /r/ (raining).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, hot, cold).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks "What is the weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answers "It is [weather]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expresses feelings "I'm hot/cold" with gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can count weather features (e.g., clouds, raindrops) up to 5-10.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Each student draws a smiley face, straight face, or sad face on a small paper for each of these questions:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'It is sunny'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I ask 'What is the weather today?'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (Teacher prompts: "Sunny? Rainy? Cold? Hot?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to talk about the weather with your friend?" (Students can show thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, paired activities, counting). The use of the "Weather Reporter" game engaged most students and provided ample speaking opportunities. The focus on /s/ and /r/ pronunciation was effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? Some students still confused "hot" and "cold" or struggled with number words beyond 10. For writing, I need to provide sentence frames and focus on single-word spelling first, then simple sentences. Ensure clear visual prompts for writing activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today, students practiced speaking about the weather. In the next lesson, they will use these spoken phrases and vocabulary to write simple sentences about the weather, connecting spoken words to their written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>